      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Phase separation in liquid cleaning products occurs when the surfactant or emulsifier system can no longer maintain a stable, homogeneous mixture of the product's aqueous and oil or polymer components. The most common triggers are temperature excursions (storage above or below the formulation's stability range), insufficient emulsifier concentration, incompatible surfactant combinations, or changes in electrolyte content that disrupt micellar structure. The upper and lower phases represent the product's components partitioning to their thermodynamically preferred environments — typically a surfactant-rich or oil-rich phase above and an aqueous electrolyte phase below. Once phase separation has occurred, the product is usually non-recoverable to its original stable state without reformulation." }
