      <section id="prevention">
        <h2>Prevention: Process Controls and Formulation Strategies to Avoid Recurrence</h2>
        <p>
          The most effective approach to cleaning product phase separation is prevention
          through systematic stability screening during formulation development — before
          scale-up, before packaging lock-down, and before distribution logistics are
          committed. A formulation that passes ambient appearance testing but has not been
          subjected to thermal stress, centrifuge, and electrolyte sensitivity screening
          cannot be considered stability-qualified, regardless of how well it performs at
          room temperature. Prevention requires both formulation-level decisions and
          manufacturing process controls to be aligned.
        </p>

        <h3>Formulation-Level Prevention</h3>
        <p>
          Map the stability boundary of every formulation during development: construct the
          ternary phase diagram for the key components (surfactant, water, electrolyte) and
          identify the composition ranges that produce stable isotropic phases across the
          full anticipated storage temperature range. Choose surfactant grades — particularly
          nonionic grades — with cloud points confirmed to be at least 10°C below the
          lowest anticipated storage temperature. Select process water with defined and
          controlled hardness — soft or de-ionised water — and tighten incoming quality
          acceptance criteria for salt impurity content in anionic surfactant raw materials.
          For any formulation containing a dispersed oil or solvent phase, validate the
          emulsifier system at pilot scale before committing to production.
        </p>

        <h3>Manufacturing Process Controls</h3>
        <p>
          Temperature control during manufacturing is critical: many liquid cleaner
          formulations are thermodynamically stable at the manufacturing temperature but
          pass through a liquid crystal phase boundary during cooling to ambient or
          filling temperature. Controlled cooling rate — rather than rapid cooling —
          allows the system to equilibrate uniformly rather than locking in locally
          phase-separated microstructure. Homogenisation shear energy must be validated
          for each batch size and mixer configuration at scale-up, as under-homogenised
          emulsions pass laboratory testing but separate in the higher fill volumes of
          production containers. Retain samples from every production batch under
          accelerated storage conditions (50°C/4 weeks) as an ongoing QC check on
          formulation consistency. The
          <a href="https://ec.europa.eu/environment/ecolabel/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU Ecolabel criteria for household cleaning products</a>
          include explicit stability requirements at temperature extremes — a useful
          industry benchmark for minimum stability screening scope even for products
          not pursuing ecolabel certification.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="when-to-escalate">
        <h2>When to Escalate to a Specialist</h2>
        <p>
          Phase separation problems that respond to straightforward corrective measures —
          adjusting hydrotrope concentration, switching surfactant grade, or improving
          process water quality — can usually be resolved in-house with adequate analytical
          equipment and formulation knowledge. However, certain failure patterns signal
          that the problem has exceeded the resolution capability of standard troubleshooting
          and requires specialist input.
        </p>
        <p>
          Escalation is warranted when separation occurs across multiple formulation
          variants despite standard corrections — this suggests a fundamental phase
          incompatibility in the surfactant system architecture rather than a parameter
          adjustment issue. Phase separation occurring only in specific container types
          or closure materials (indicating interaction between the product and packaging
          material) also requires specialist investigation. When separation is batch-specific
          without any obvious raw material or process change, systematic phase diagram
          mapping and raw material fingerprinting are needed — both of which require
          analytical equipment and expertise beyond standard QC capability. Finally, if
          the product is destined for regulated markets (biocidal products, industrial
          cleaning under specific safety standards), phase separation during regulatory
          stability studies may require formal re-qualification, which carries regulatory
          as well as formulation dimensions. Our
          <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/choosing-the-right-product-consultant" target="_top">guide to choosing a product consultant</a>
          describes the evaluation criteria for selecting specialist support.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <section class="faq-section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does my cleaning product separate into two layers during storage?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Phase separation in liquid cleaning products occurs when the surfactant or emulsifier system can no longer maintain a stable, homogeneous mixture of the product's aqueous and oil or polymer components. The most common triggers are temperature excursions (storage above or below the formulation's stability range), insufficient emulsifier concentration, incompatible surfactant combinations, or changes in electrolyte content that disrupt micellar structure. The upper and lower phases represent the product's components partitioning to their thermodynamically preferred environments — typically a surfactant-rich or oil-rich phase above and an aqueous electrolyte phase below. Once phase separation has occurred, the product is usually non-recoverable to its original stable state without reformulation.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What causes a liquid detergent to separate in cold weather?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Cold temperature separation in liquid detergents is most commonly caused by surfactant crystallisation or liquid crystal phase formation below the product's stability threshold. Nonionic surfactants — including alkyl ethoxylates and alkyl polyglucosides — have defined cloud points and pour points below which their solubility in the aqueous phase decreases sharply, causing them to separate as a cloudy or waxy phase. In anionic-nonionic blends, the nonionic component may crystallise out preferentially at low temperature, disrupting the balance of the emulsifier system and allowing the remaining components to separate. Formulators address this by selecting surfactant grades with lower cloud points, blending surfactants to depress the effective cloud point, or incorporating hydrotropes to maintain solubility across the required temperature range.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How do I diagnose whether phase separation is temperature-driven or formulation-driven?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The key diagnostic test is accelerated thermal cycling: subject a sample to the suspected temperature extreme (e.g. 0°C for 24 hours, or 50°C for 48 hours) and observe whether phase separation occurs and whether it reverses on return to ambient temperature. If separation is reversible on warming and the product homogenises with gentle agitation, it is likely a low-temperature solubility issue with the surfactant system. If separation is irreversible — the product does not return to a uniform state — the failure is structural: the emulsion system has collapsed and the dispersed phase has coalesced irreversibly. Chemical analysis of each separated phase (surfactant composition, pH, electrolyte content) confirms whether the separation is compositional stratification or true emulsion inversion.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can a separated cleaning product be fixed by shaking the bottle?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Gentle shaking may temporarily rehomogenise a product that has undergone mild reversible separation — for example, a nonionic surfactant system that has gelled slightly at low temperature but has not yet lost its emulsification capacity. However, if coalescence of the dispersed phase has occurred — visible as distinct oily globules floating on the surface or large clear or oily pools at the top — shaking will not restore the original emulsion. The droplet size distribution has increased beyond the point where the existing emulsifier concentration can stabilise it again. For quality assurance purposes, any separated product should be failed and not released to market, even if it appears to rehomogenise, as its storage stability and performance will be compromised.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What is the role of hydrotropes in preventing phase separation?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Hydrotropes — water-soluble compounds such as sodium cumene sulphonate, sodium xylene sulphonate, and sodium toluene sulphonate — improve the solubility of surfactants and other poorly water-soluble components in aqueous cleaning formulations. They function by disrupting the ordered micellar structures that form at high surfactant concentrations and preventing the formation of viscous liquid crystal phases that can stratify during storage. In high-surfactant-load formulations such as concentrated liquid detergents and hard surface cleaners, hydrotropes are essential for maintaining a single-phase, pourable product across the storage temperature range. Without an adequate hydrotrope level, concentrated anionic formulations in particular tend to form highly viscous lamellar liquid crystal phases that separate from the dilute aqueous phase on standing.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>When should I escalate a phase separation problem to a formulation specialist?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Escalation is warranted when phase separation persists after the straightforward corrective steps — verifying surfactant levels, adjusting hydrotrope concentration, and checking electrolyte balance — have been attempted without resolving the problem. Complex cases requiring specialist input include separation at intermediate temperatures pointing to a liquid crystal phase boundary problem; separation in one batch but not others suggesting a raw material quality issue; products where the phase diagram has not been mapped across the full composition space; and emulsified cleaning products where multiple destabilisation mechanisms may be operating simultaneously. A formulation specialist will construct the ternary phase diagram, evaluate surfactant HLB optimisation, and run systematic stability screens across the full temperature and concentration range.
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>

